[DONE] Some creatures (especially gaunts/sixfold/etc) tend to be REALLY hard to right-click, especially after they're knocked unconscious. It's almost a guessing game of where to click because it doesn't seem consistent with the body itself.
    It was not a guessing game. It was PARALLAX, and the arithmetic is brutal.
    Every pick in the file compared the cursor to a body's TILE — `dist(o.x,
    o.y, wx, wy) < 0.9` and a dozen variants — but `wx, wy` comes out of
    `screenToWorld`, which follows the ray down until it meets the GROUND. So a
    click aimed at a creature resolves to the dirt BEHIND it, by

        drift = height / tan(camPitch)

    Measured in the running game at the default pitch of 0.95: aiming at the
    middle of an ordinary person lands 0.61 tiles behind their feet, and at a
    Sixfold 1.54. The catchment was 0.8-0.9. So an ordinary person just barely
    worked, which is exactly why the report names the big ones — and tilting the
    camera to the 0.42 the controls allow takes those to 2.0 and 4.9 tiles, at
    which point nothing works at all. There was never a spot ON the creature
    that was correct; the correct spot was some ground behind it that MOVED as
    the player turned the camera. Hence "a guessing game", precisely.
    THE SECOND HALF IS SIZE. A Sixfold is 2.4 times a man and a grown Cairn
    Beast is eight, and every radius in the file was written for a man.
    AND THE UNCONSCIOUS CASE IS THE SAME BUG'S OTHER FACE, from the other side.
    A body on the ground has almost no height, so its drift is almost nothing —
    the compensation the player had learned for the standing creature silently
    stopped applying. What is wrong with a downed one is SPRAWL: at 2.4x it lies
    across better than two tiles and the catchment was nine tenths of one, at
    its middle.
    Fixed as `bodyGap`/`bodyHit`: work out where a click aimed at the middle of
    a body actually lands, and accept EITHER that point or the feet — accepting
    both can only make a click land that would have missed, so nobody who
    learned to aim low is punished. The catchment grows with `big`, and is
    NEUTRAL AT BIG 1, so every ordinary person in the game is picked exactly as
    before and only the drift changes for them. 33 hit-tests and 7 nearest-body
    comparisons converted, so the sort that picks between two bodies under a
    crowded cursor uses the same measure that let either be picked at all.
    Proof in tools/clicks.js.
    WHY NO EXISTING HARNESS SAW IT, which is the part worth keeping: both
    tools/rightclick.js and tools/wanderers.js drive the real handler at real
    screen coordinates, and both aim at `groundY(t.x, t.y) + 0.05` — the body's
    FEET. A ray aimed at the ground lands exactly where it was aimed. They were
    testing the menus, correctly, through the one aim point in the whole system
