    carries them seventeen tiles under a real move order, and sets them down.
    THERE WERE THREE CARRY MECHANISMS AND NONE OF THEM WAS THIS ONE.
      · `carryTarget` drops the order unless `corpses.includes(t)` — it will not
        lift anything still alive.
      · `rescueTarget` is narrower than its name and only cuts somebody out of a
        midden (`v.heldAt`).
      · `seizeTarget` does lift a downed living body, and then hauls it to a
        Holding Cell and sets `captured` — the flag the whole world reads as
        "prisoner". That is precisely the kidnapping the note rules out.
    SO THE RULE IS ABOUT THE DESTINATION, WHICH IS WHAT THE NOTE SAYS, and it is
    enforced by construction rather than by a check: SEIZE is offered on a body
    you are already fighting and sets `seizeTarget`; PICK UP is offered on
    anybody on the ground and sets `bearTarget`. Nothing reachable from a
    non-hostile can end in a cell, and the harness asserts that too.
    THE BODY HAS TO FOLLOW, and that is what made this a feature rather than a
    menu entry. The slaver haul and the larder haul each pin their captive beside
    them by hand, inline in their own branches. The player's own seize never did
